Subject: [PATCH] ole32: do not hold a reference on the registered drop target

RegisterDragDrop() takes a reference on the caller's IDropTarget and stores
the pointer in the "OleDropTargetInterface" window prop; RevokeDragDrop()
then dereferences that pointer and releases it.

Windows does neither.  Verified statically against ole32.dll 10.0.26100.8875
(Windows 11 build 26200):

  * RegisterDragDrop stores the pointer with SetPropW and takes no reference
    (the register path is "mov r8,rsi / call SetPropW", with no AddRef on
    the caller-supplied interface anywhere in the function);

  * RevokeDragDrop reads it back with GetPropW and only tests it against
    NULL -- "call GetPropW / test rax,rax / je" and rax is never used
    afterwards.  The Release calls in that function operate on an object
    ole32 obtains internally, not on the stored pointer.

So on Windows the prop is a weak, non-owning pointer.  Applications may
therefore free their drop target before calling RevokeDragDrop, and some do
-- ole2.c already acknowledges this in the DropTargetWrapper comment ("some
apps destroy this prior to CoUninitialize without calling RevokeDragDrop").
Under Wine the same sequence leaves the prop dangling and RevokeDragDrop
faults reading the vtable:

  RevokeDragDrop+0x58:  mov  rdx,[rax]     ; rax = GetPropW(...)
                        mov  rcx,rax
                        call [rdx+0x10]    ; IUnknown::Release

Reproduced with a Tauri/WebView2 application (SWG Infinity's Infinity
Launcher) on wine-11.16-staging-tkg-amd64-wow64: reliable crash during
startup window teardown.  Faulting data addresses vary per run under ASLR
but always land in freed heap.

Drop the AddRef/Release pair so the prop matches Windows' weak-pointer
semantics.  get_target_from_wrapper() is unaffected -- it takes its own
temporary reference for the duration of each call, which remains correct
while the prop is valid.

Build integration verified against dwproton Wine commit
299693a0002d839eac479514b4ef44de19bf73a9.  Runtime behaviour of the resulting
complete custom runner still requires an end-to-end SWG Deck smoke test.

---
--- a/dlls/ole32/ole2.c
+++ b/dlls/ole32/ole2.c
@@ -605,7 +605,11 @@
     hr = create_map_from_stream(stream, &map);
     if(SUCCEEDED(hr))
     {
-      IDropTarget_AddRef(pDropTarget);
+      /* Windows stores the caller's target in the prop without
+       * taking a reference, and never releases it in
+       * RevokeDragDrop.  Apps rely on that: some free their
+       * target before revoking, which is safe there.  Holding a
+       * ref here makes the prop dangle for those apps. */
       SetPropW(hwnd, prop_oledroptarget, pDropTarget);
       SetPropW(hwnd, prop_marshalleddroptarget, map);
     }
@@ -629,7 +633,6 @@
 {
   HANDLE map;
   IStream *stream;
-  IDropTarget *drop_target;
   HRESULT hr;
 
   TRACE("(%p)\n", hwnd);
@@ -644,9 +647,9 @@
   if (!(map = get_droptarget_handle(hwnd)))
     return DRAGDROP_E_NOTREGISTERED;
 
-  drop_target = GetPropW(hwnd, prop_oledroptarget);
-  if(drop_target) IDropTarget_Release(drop_target);
-
+  /* No reference was taken in RegisterDragDrop, so nothing to
+   * release here.  Dereferencing the stored pointer would fault
+   * for apps that already freed their target. */
   RemovePropW(hwnd, prop_oledroptarget);
   RemovePropW(hwnd, prop_marshalleddroptarget);
 
